"PSA: Chrome is moving to libc++ (https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/chromium-os-dev/c/e8pJRHLisIg)\nciteturn25view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/chromium-os-dev/c/e8pJRHLisIg\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 141\nL0: cite0†Groups L1: \nL2: Groups\nL3: \nL4: [Input][Input]\nL5: \nL6: cite1†Sign in†accounts.google.com L7: \nL8: cite0†Groups L9: \nL10: Groups\nL11: \nL12: ## ChromiumOS Development\nL13: \nL14: cite2†Conversations L15: \nL16: cite3†About L17: \nL18: Send feedback\nL19: \nL20: Help\nL21: \nL22: cite4†Privacy†policies.google.com • cite5†Terms†policies.google.com L23: \nL24: \nL25: \nL26: \nL27: \nL28: \nL29: \nL30: \nL31: \nL32: # PSA: Chrome is moving to libc++\nL33: \nL34: 347 views\nL35: \nL36: Skip to first unread message\nL37: \nL38: \nL39: \nL40: cite6†Image: Manoj Gupta's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com L41: ### Manoj Gupta\nL42: \nL43: unread,\nL44: \nL45: Oct 20, 2017, 2:54:24 PM 10/20/17\nL46: \nL47: \nL48: \nL49: \nL50: \nL51: \nL52: \nL53: to Chromium OS dev\nL54: \nL55: Hi,\nL56: \nL57: ChromeOS toolchain team plans to migrate Chrome on ChromeOS early next week to be built with LLVM’s cite7†libc++†libcxx.llvm.org instead of GCC’s libstdc++ (cite8†CL:717537†chromium-review.googlesource.com ).\nL58: \nL59: This is a part of our plan to bring C++14 support to Chrome and will also enable security features including  cite9†CFI†clang.llvm.org (Control-Flow Integrity) that we plan to deploy in future.\nL60: Important note [USER ACTION MAY BE REQUIRED!!]::\nL61: \nL62: SimpleChrome developers may have to remove their old Out directories in case  they run into linker errors as described below.\nL63: \nL64: In addition, anyone relying on an incremental Chrome build in chroot may also have to clean the old Out directories.\nL65: \nL66: Details:\nL67: \nL68: Libc++ is NOT ABI compatible with libstdc++. Therefore, libstdc++/libc++ compiled objects may NOT be intermixed.\nL69: \nL70: The linker will produce errors when such a situation happens.\nL71: For instance, when  foo.o (compiled with libstdc++) using std::string and std::cout  and main.o (compiled with libc++) are mixed,\nL72: \nL73: Linker raises undefined reference errors:\nL74: \nL75: main.o:main.cpp:function main: error: undefined reference to 'foo(std::__1::basic_string<char, std::__1::char_traits<char>, std::__1::allocator<char> >&)'  data.o:data.cpp:function foo(std::string&):\nL76: \nL77:         error: undefined reference to 'std::cout'\nL78: \nL79: data.o:data.cpp:function foo(std::string&):\nL80:         error: undefined reference to 'std::ostream::operator<<(std::ostream& (*)(std::ostream&))\nL81: \nL82: If you discover any issues related to this migration, please report them at\nL83: \nL84: cite10†crosbug.com†crosbug.com (use label \"Build-Toolchain\"), or email chromeos-toolchain@google.com.\nL85: \nL86: Thanks,\nL87: \nL88: Manoj Gupta\nL89: \nL90: (ChromeOS toolchain)\nL91: \nL92: cite6†Image: Manoj Gupta's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com L93: ### Manoj Gupta\nL94: \nL95: unread,\nL96: \nL97: Oct 25, 2017, 2:10:42 PM 10/25/17\nL98: \nL99: \nL100: \nL101: \nL102: \nL103: \nL104: \nL105: to Chromium OS dev\nL106: \nL107: The migration to libc++ has been done. Chrome/ChromeOS version starting from chromeos-chrome-64.0.3249.0 are built with libc++.\nL108: \nL109: Please report any issues at cite10†crosbug.com†crosbug.com (use label \"Build-Toolchain\"), or email chromeos-toolchain@google.com.\nL110: \nL111: Thanks,\nL112: \nL113: Manoj\nL114: \nL115: \nL116: \nL117: Reply all\nL118: \nL119: Reply to author\nL120: \nL121: Forward\nL122: \nL123: This conversation is locked\nL124: \nL125: You cannot reply and perform actions on locked conversations.\nL126: \nL127: \nL128: 0 new messages\nL129: \nL130: \nL131: \nL132: Search\nL133: \nL134: Clear search\nL135: \nL136: Close search\nL137: \nL138: Google apps\nL139: \nL140: Main menu\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nabsl::Mutex in Chromium? (https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/cxx/c/bIlGr1URn8I)\nciteturn25view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/cxx/c/bIlGr1URn8I\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1110\nL0: cite0†Groups L1: \nL2: Groups\nL3: \nL4: [Input][Input]\nL5: \nL6: cite1†Sign in†accounts.google.com L7: \nL8: cite0†Groups L9: \nL10: Groups\nL11: \nL12: ## cxx\nL13: \nL14: cite2†Conversations L15: \nL16: cite3†About L17: \nL18: Send feedback\nL19: \nL20: Help\nL21: \nL22: cite4†Privacy†policies.google.com • cite5†Terms†policies.google.com L23: \nL24: \nL25: \nL26: \nL27: \nL28: \nL29: \nL30: \nL31: \nL32: # absl::Mutex in Chromium?\nL33: \nL34: 705 views\nL35: \nL36: Skip to first unread message\nL37: \nL38: \nL39: \nL40: cite6†Image: Alexei Svitkine's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com L41: ### Alexei Svitkine\nL42: \nL43: unread,\nL44: \nL45: Oct 12, 2021, 5:42:59 PM 10/12/21\nL46: \nL47: \nL48: \nL49: \nL50: \nL51: \nL52: \nL53: to cxx\nL54: \nL55: Hello,\nL56: \nL57: As part of my investigation on cite7†crbug.com/1123627†crbug.com , I'm interested in using a Read/Write lock in base::StatisticsRecorder.\nL58: \nL59: It looks like absl::Mutex fits this case - which is not supported by base::Lock. Amusingly, the Chromium site page has sample code on how to \"roll your own\" R/W lock:\nL60: \nL61: cite8†https://www.chromium.org/developers/lock-and-condition-variable†www.chromium.org L62: I assume we can all agree that rolling your own r/w lock doesn't seem ideal. What would be the process for getting approved to use absl::Mutex in this specific case?\nL63: \nL64: As an aside, C++17 will introduce std::shared_mutex, which also handles this case:\nL65: \nL66: cite9†https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/thread/shared_mutex†en.cppreference.com L67: \nL68: But it looks like we're not on C++17 yet?\nL69: \nL70: (Apologies if there's a better place to discuss absl than cxx@ - let me know.)\nL71: \nL72: -Alexei\nL73: cite10†Image: K. Moon's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com L74: ### K. Moon\nL75: \nL76: unread,\nL77: \nL78: Oct 12, 2021, 6:33:19 PM 10/12/21\nL79: \nL80: \nL81: \nL82: \nL83: \nL84: \nL85: \nL86: to Alexei Svitkine, cxx\nL87: \nL88: Yep, this is the designated place to discuss Abseil. I recall mutex implementations and reader-writer locks in particular are controversial, though, so this probably will be a long discussion...\nL89: \nL90: \nL91: \nL92: \nL93: > --\nL94: > You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups \"cxx\" group.\nL95: > To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to cxx+uns...@chromium.org.\nL96: > To view this discussion on the web visit cite11†https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/cxx/CAKFU2SAJg%2By4FUU-qTWkhq1XAeCWB2GKo%2BjSUbkMFdVK%2Bakxcg%40mail.gmail.com .\nL97: \nL98: cite10†Image: K. Moon's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com L99: ### K. Moon\nL100: \nL101: unread,\nL102: \nL103: Oct 12, 2021, 6:38:22 PM 10/12/21\nL104: \nL105: \nL106: \nL107: \nL108: \nL109: \nL110: \nL111: to Alexei Svitkine, cxx\nL112: \nL113: As an example, internal TotW #197 cautions against using the reader lock mechanism in performance-sensitive code. Your example seems like the situation the TotW is warning against.\nL114: \nL115: \nL116: \nL117: cite12†Image: Peter Kasting's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com L118: ### Peter Kasting\nL119: \nL120: unread,\nL121: \nL122: Oct 12, 2021, 9:48:19 PM 10/12/21\nL123: \nL124: \nL125: \nL126: \nL127: \nL128: \nL129: \nL130: to K. Moon, Alexei Svitkine, cxx\nL131: \nL132: IMO, better to have a dangerous tool than no tool.\nL133: \nL134: PK\nL135: \nL136: cite13†Image: Peter Boström's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com L137: ### Peter Boström\nL138: \nL139: unread,\nL140: \nL141: Oct 12, 2021, 10:34:40 PM 10/12/21\nL142: \nL143: \nL144: \nL145: \nL146: \nL147: \nL148: \nL149: to Peter Kasting, Alexei Svitkine, K. Moon, cxx\nL150: \nL151: Would the \"no tool\" here would just be replacing rw locks with regular mutexes or everyone rolls their own rw locks?\nL152: \nL153: On Tue, Oct 12, 2021 at 6:48 PM 'Peter Kasting' via cxx <c...@chromium.org> wrote:\nL154: \nL155: > IMO, better to have a dangerous tool than no tool.\nL156: >\nL157: > PK\nL158: \nL159: > --\nL160: > You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups \"cxx\" group.\nL161: > To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to cxx+uns...@chromium.org.\nL162: \nL163: > To view this discussion on the web visit cite14†https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/cxx/CAAHOzFDX6Kj9wjSThGh1U3rHjAL_HDhicS2mh3YpUYwVUCnWpw%40mail.gmail.com .\nL164: \nL165: cite12†Image: Peter Kasting's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com L166: ### Peter Kasting\nL167: \nL168: unread,\nL169: \nL170: Oct 13, 2021, 7:23:25 AM 10/13/21\nL171: \nL172: \nL173: \nL174: \nL175: \nL176: \nL177: \nL178: to Peter Boström, Alexei Svitkine, K. Moon, cxx\nL179: \nL180: On Tue, Oct 12, 2021 at 7:34 PM Peter Boström <pb...@chromium.org> wrote:\nL181: \nL182: > Would the \"no tool\" here would just be replacing rw locks with regular mutexes or everyone rolls their own rw locks?\nL183: \nL184: Either way, I think?\nL185: \nL186: PK\nL187: \nL188: cite6†Image: Alexei Svitkine's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com L189: ### Alexei Svitkine\nL190: \nL191: unread,\nL192: \nL193: Oct 13, 2021, 9:14:49 AM 10/13/21\nL194: \nL195: \nL196: \nL197: \nL198: \nL199: \nL200: \nL201: to Peter Kasting, Peter Boström, K. Moon, cxx\nL202: \nL203: Thanks for the pointer to the TOTW, I haven't seen it.\nL204: \nL205: To summarize, the argument against R/W lock is that the fixed cost of acquiring/releasing it is higher than a regular lock, so it may actually hurt performance - and provides some general guidance to avoid it for short-lived lock operations.\nL206: That's a fair point, though in the case where I'm interested in using it, it's actually not \"performance sensitive code\". This probably sounds surprising - since why would I even care about changing the lock if it's not performance critical? Let me explain.\nL207: 1. The lock in question is the one used to look up Histogram objects in a global map. This is needed when a histogram is logged (thousands of call sites in Chromium), BUT not all callsites are equally affected. In particular, histograms logged via histogram_functions.h APIs do the look up (and thus acquire the lock) each time, whereas call sites using histogram_macros.h actually only look up the histogram once, at the first call, and cache in a function-scoped static - so only interact with the lock once.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    \n    \n      \n    \n    \n      Issue 2965693002: Revert of Switch Chrome on Linux to libc++ -\n    \n    Code Review\n   (https://codereview.chromium.org/2965693002)\nciteturn25view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://codereview.chromium.org/2965693002\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 316\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n⚙ D150897 [runtimes] Use LLVM libunwind from libc++abi and compiler-rt by default (https://reviews.llvm.org/D150897)\nciteturn25view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://reviews.llvm.org/D150897\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 392\n"
